# Container Watchdog — watched containers
#
# One record per line. Every token is KEY=VALUE and the first token must be
# container=. A malformed line is skipped and logged; it is never guessed at.
# Nothing is watched until you add records here.
#
#   container=NAME       Exact container name. Required.
#   checks=LIST          Comma separated: running, health, network, ports, http.
#                        Default: running,health,network,ports
#   action=LEVEL         notify   report only, never touch the container
#                        restart  may start a stopped container and restart a
#                                 broken one
#                        reattach everything restart does, plus reconnecting a
#                                 container that lost its Docker network
#                        Default: notify
#   networks=LIST        Comma separated networks that must stay attached.
#                        Required when action=reattach.
#   threshold=N          Consecutive failed cycles before acting. Default 3.
#   cooldown=SECONDS     Minimum gap between repairs. Default 600, minimum 60.
#   max_actions=N        Repairs allowed per window before the container is
#                        suspended into notify-only. Default 3.
#   window=SECONDS       Length of that rolling window. Default 3600.
#   http=URL             Optional probe. Only used when checks includes http.
#   http_status=CODE     Expected status for that probe. Default 200.
#
# The checks are deliberately cheap and read-only. The network and ports checks
# exist because a container can report healthy while having no interface beyond
# loopback: its health probe passes over localhost while every outbound call
# fails and no published port is reachable. A plain restart does not repair that
# state, which is why reattach exists.
#
# A container latched by Container Breakglass, or covered by an armed deployment
# guard, is reported but never touched regardless of the action set here.

# ── Examples ──
#
# RedMan lost its bridge attachment after an unclean boot and stayed "healthy"
# for two days while every peer call failed. Full checks plus reattach.
#
# container=redman action=reattach networks=redman-docker-api
#
# Its sidecars share the same network but publish no ports.
#
# container=redman-docker-socket-proxy action=restart checks=running,network networks=redman-docker-api
# container=redman-docker-control-proxy action=restart checks=running,network networks=redman-docker-api
#
# Nextcloud AIO manages its own child containers. Watch the master container,
# and keep the children on notify so the watchdog never fights the manager.
#
# container=nextcloud-aio-mastercontainer action=restart
# container=nextcloud-aio-nextcloud action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-database action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-apache action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-redis action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-notify-push action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-collabora action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-imaginary action=notify
# container=nextcloud-aio-whiteboard action=notify
#
# A service that is known to flap should stay on notify, otherwise the watchdog
# spends its repair budget on a problem it cannot fix.
#
# container=nextcloud-aio-fulltextsearch action=notify
#
# Photo and backup services: restart is safe, they have no external manager.
#
# container=Immich action=restart
# container=Immich_PostgreSQL action=restart
# container=TimeMachine action=restart checks=running
#
# Dashboards and tunnels. A tunnel client is worth an HTTP probe because a dead
# tunnel usually keeps its process alive.
#
# container=homepage action=restart http=http://127.0.0.1:3000/ checks=running,health,network,ports,http
# container=newt action=restart checks=running
# container=wireguard-client action=restart checks=running
